140
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:27,000
Yeah, yeah, get John Cook Company expense, perfect.

141
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:30,000
Or some sort of team challenge, get everyone pulling together.

142
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:32,000
Maybe in the daytime first.

143
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:42,000
Yeah, what about go-karting? Is that a team challenge? What about party pilates? What? Darts. Guys like darts. Everyone's a winner.

144
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:44,000
That's quite a good idea, actually.

145
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:45,000
Yeah, and I like darts.

146
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:46,000
No, I don't like darts.

147
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:50,000
I think we should send a questionnaire deciding what motivates the team.

148
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:51,000
Darts.

149
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:52,000
No, no, I might kill someone.

150
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:54,000
I'm glad you consider everyone, Jason.

151
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:57,000
OK, so we'll ask the staff, then we'll put it to a vote.

152
00:09:57,000 --> 00:09:58,000
Yeah, great idea.

153
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:03,000
So we'll give them some options, put it to a vote, and then there'll be no excuse for any complaints.

154
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:05,000
OK, time's ticking on.

155
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:19,000
Can we all have a think? Come up with some ideas for the next meeting, and then at the meeting we can decide on about six and put it to a company vote, yeah? Why don't we decide on those things now while they're still fresh in everybody's minds? Yeah, and those issues do need addressing now.

156
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:29,000
OK, so what have we got? We have go-karting, awards night, party for lattes, darts, and drinking evening.

157
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:31,000
We need more than that.

158
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:36,000
Let's all come up with four and email them over in the next two days, and I'll take it from there.

159
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:39,000
So, moving on, yes, IT issues.

160
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:46,000
So what are the IT issues? I heard there was a problem on Friday, and I was in St Albans with a new client.

161
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:50,000
Friday's issues took me quite a long time to investigate.

162
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:53,000
In fact, it took me pretty much all of my afternoon.

163
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:59,000
The F drive backup recovery disassociated itself somehow from the PCP main framework.

164
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:05,000
I found that this was a problem dating back to the beginning of last month when the PCP was originally reinstalled.

165
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:11,000
Somehow, quite a large component from the logitive rebix hadn't been installed in the reinstallation.

166
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:19,000
Indeed, this was also a problem with the external energy source administration detail being overlooked.

167
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:25,000
Frank, just try explaining that back to us as if we're five-year-olds, would you? Oh, jeez.

168
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:33,000
Shall I continue? There was an external energy issue preventing us from powering up our mainframe and sub-feeders.

169
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:38,000
Do you mean there was a power cut, Frank? Yes, in simple terms.

170
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:40,000
Thank you.

171
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:46,000
So how can we avoid this happening again? I guess it would be a matter for contacting the electricity supplier.

172
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:48,000
Pay the electricity bill? Yes.

173
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:54,000
Can we minute accounts please pay the electricity bills on time? Agreed? Agreed.

174
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:58,000
And maybe we should have sent Frank on an effective communication skills course.

175
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:00,000
Agreed, yeah.

176
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:02,000
Right, moving swiftly on.

177
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:09,000
What about the training issue? Oh, I've been thinking about that and I've got an idea but I want to run it past Clive first.

178
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:13,000
Maybe we could save that for the next meeting? Oh.

179
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:22,000
Okay, but I mean I think we should have that meeting sooner rather than later, maybe in the next two weeks? Okay, but not everybody needs to be there.

180
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:26,000
I'd like myself and Clive and Frank as it's relevant to them.

181
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:29,000
But I think I should be there as well.

182
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:32,000
That's my final decision, Sue, thank you.

183
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:35,000
I'll speak with Clive and let you know a date, Frank.

184
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:46,000
Moving on, yes, Gary couldn't attend today so he's provided us with a financial report which in summary, well, we've had a tough year.

185
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:53,000
The balance sheet's about even and we're doing pretty well in contract sales so thanks very much for that.

186
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:58,000
On the downside, loss of revenue in handset sales abroad.

187
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:02,000
How much are we down on last year? I mean Gary's layout here is quite confusing.

188
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:09,000
This figure here, 52 or 21? I mean, I'm not familiar with this kind of financial report.

189
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:11,000
He seems to have changed it.

190
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:13,000
That's a big difference. Let me just have a look.

191
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:15,000
I've got a bit of accounting experience.

192
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:17,000
Thanks, Julian.

193
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:19,000
You know, that's a lot.

194
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:21,000
Seriously, I mean, what could you possibly...

195
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:23,000
It's actually neither of those numbers, it's 11.

196
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:28,000
Are we in red or black? Well, I think if we can't pay our utility bill, I think we're in the red.

197
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:31,000
Actually, we are in the black, but we're down on last year.

198
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:33,000
Okay, well, thank you for that.

199
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:35,000
I think that's about all for this meeting.

200
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:37,000
